# Designing Classes

# Abstract Data Types

**Q**: An implementation of an ADT with a programming language is called a/an:
a. Abstract Data Type
b. Data Structure
c. Collection
d. Primitive

<details><summary>Answer</summary>
b. Data Structure
</details>

**Q**: To accommodate entries of any class, the *Bag* interface and implementation use __ in Java.
a. An inherited type
b. A generic type
c. A sub class type
d. All of the above

<details><summary>Answer</summary>
b. A generic type
</details>

**Q**: When using abstraction as a design principle you should focus on:
a. What you want to do with the data
b. How the data is stored
c. How the data is manipulated
d. All of the above

<details><summary>Answer</summary>
a. What you want to do with the data
</details>

**Q**: A language-independent specification for a group of values and operations on those values is called a/an:
a. Collection
b. Data Structure
c. Abstract Data Type
d. Primitive

<details><summary>Answer</summary>
b. Data Structure
</details>

